ssions, check for version drift (unpinned dependencies, recent bumps) and verify with logs/Honeycomb/DB queries.\\n- State hypotheses tentatively and validate with evidence before committing to a fix direction.\",\n        \"why\": \"Multiple sessions show Claude over-committing to wrong theories (mini-model hallucination, error-path theory) that users pushed back on; the real causes were unpinned versions and other concrete issues found via telemetry.\",\n        \"prompt_scaffold\": \"Add under a ## Debugging section, or create one if missing.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"addition\": \"## Go Conventions\\n- Go time format reference string is `2006-01-02 15:04:05` (NOT 2026). Double-check time formatting constants before use.\\n- Preserve `//nolint` directives during lint/fmt cycles; do not strip them.\",\n        \"why\": \"Claude used 2026 instead of 2006 for Go time format, and lint cycles repeatedly stripped nolint comments requiring multiple restoration attempts.\",\n        \"prompt_scaffold\": \"Add under a ## Go Conventions or ## Language Notes section.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"addition\": \"## Contributor Classification\\nWhen generating changelogs or release notes, do not classify contributors as external solely based on git email domain. Verify against the org's known contributor list.\",\n        \"why\": \"Claude misclassified @ashtom as external because his git email wasn't @entire.io, requiring user correction in the changelog skill.\",\n        \"prompt_scaffold\": \"Add under a ## Release Notes / Changelog section.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"features_to_try\": [\n      {\n        \"feature\": \"Custom Skills\",\n        \"one_liner\": \"Reusable markdown prompts invoked via /command\",\n        \"why_for_you\": \"You ran 17 code reviews and used existing skills like /review and /changelog heavily. Codifying your common flows (/pr-review with diff+context fetch, /branch-review, /simplify with scope guard) would save repeat instructions and prevent issues like scope-creep into sibling repos.\",\n        \"example_code\": \"mkdir -p .claude/skills/branch-review && cat > .claude/skills/branch-review/SKILL.md <<'EOF'\\n---\\nname: branch-review\\ndescription: Review the current branch's diff vs main\\n---\\n1. Run `git diff main...HEAD` and `git log main..HEAD --oneline`\\n2. Only review files in THIS repo (never ../sibling/*)\\n3. Produce a structured review: Correctness, Tests, Style, Risks\\n4. Reference specific file:line locations\\nEOF\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"feature\": \"Hooks\",\n        \"one_liner\": \"Auto-run shell commands at lifecycle events\",\n        \"why_for_you\": \"You had repeated CI failures (trailing-slash test paths, stats.ts formatting, schema drift) that a PostToolUse hook running fmt/lint/test on edited files would catch before commit. Would also prevent the nolint-stripping issue if you guard the format step.\",\n        \"example_code\": \"// .claude/settings.json\\n{\\n  \\\"hooks\\\": {\\n    \\\"PostToolUse\\\": [{\\n      \\\"matcher\\\": \\\"Edit|Write\\\",\\n      \\\"hooks\\\": [{\\\"type\\\": \\\"command\\\", \\\"command\\\": \\\"gofmt -w . && golangci-lint run --fix ./... 2>&1 | head -50\\\"}]\\n    }]\\n  }\\n}\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"feature\": \"MCP Servers\",\n        \"one_liner\": \"Connect Claude to GitHub, Honeycomb, and your DB directly\",\n        \"why_for_you\": \"You debug via Honeycomb+DB queries (checkpoint debugging session), do constant gh CLI work, and review PRs frequently. A GitHub MCP + Honeycomb MCP would replace dozens of bash invocations and let Claude pull PR context, comments, and traces in one structured call.\",\n        \"example_code\": \"claude mcp add github -- npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-github\\n# Then set GITHUB_TOKEN env var\\nclaude mcp add honeycomb -- npx -y @honeycombio/honeycomb-mcp\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"usage_patterns\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Lock in a PR-review checkpoint pattern\",\n        \"suggestion\": \"Your highest-value workflow is iterative branch/PR review (17 sessions). Standardize a multi-pass review skill that always re-reviews after fixes.\",\n        \"detail\": \"You frequently asked for a review, then a re-review after polish, then fixes. The checkpoint debugging session needed three review iterations to get the design right. A /review skill that explicitly outputs (1) findings, (2) suggested fixes, (3) re-review checklist would compress this loop and catch design flaws (race conditions, branch-scoped reap bugs) earlier.\",\n        \"copyable_prompt\": \"Review this branch in three passes: (1) correctness + concurrency hazards (look for races, scope-creep deletes, stale refs); (2) tests + CI signals (trailing slashes, schema parity, fmt); (3) design alternatives — list 1-2 simpler approaches before approving. Reference file:line for every finding.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Pre-commit verification ritual\",\n        \"suggestion\": \"Several commits broke CI on things Claude could have caught locally (stats.ts formatting, trailing-slash routes, schema drift, removed-helper refs).\",\n        \"detail\": \"You have 63 commits across 33 sessions. Codify a 'before-commit' checklist that runs fmt/lint/tests AND checks for stale references to removed symbols and frontend SDK regeneration when backend changes. This addresses the 'main build broken' and 'schema_drift' friction directly.\",\n        \"copyable_prompt\": \"Before committing: (1) run gofmt + golangci-lint and don't strip //nolint; (2) run the full test suite, not just touched packages; (3) grep for any references to symbols I removed in this diff; (4) if backend API/schema changed, regenerate frontend SDK; (5) report what you ran and the exit codes.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"State hypotheses tentatively, verify with telemetry\",\n        \"suggestion\": \"You pushed back on Claude's wrong theories twice (mini-model hallucination, error-path