eproduction Agent\",\n        \"whats_possible\": \"Your debugging sessions (empty Codex stub, missing checkpoint data, TTY hangs, CI regressions) all followed a pattern of hypothesis → investigation → fix, sometimes with wrong initial theories (mini-model hallucination, error-path theory). An autonomous agent could iterate against a failing test it writes first—reproducing the bug from telemetry/logs, generating a minimal failing test, then bisecting fixes against it until green, with Honeycomb/DB queries as inputs.\",\n        \"how_to_try\": \"Combine Claude Code with MCP servers for Honeycomb and your database, and instruct the agent to write a failing test before any fix attempt, looping until the test passes without disabling it.\",\n        \"copyable_prompt\": \"I'm seeing <BUG SYMPTOM> in production. Your job: (1) Query Honeycomb and the database to gather concrete evidence of the failure mode—do not theorize before checking telemetry. (2) Write a minimal failing test that reproduces the bug locally; commit it on a new branch even if it's failing. (3) Form a hypothesis, implement a fix, run the test. If it still fails, do not modify the test—iterate the fix. (4) Once green, run the full suite to check for regressions, then scan the codebase with Grep for similar patterns that might have the same bug. (5) Produce a PR with the failing-test commit followed by the fix commit, plus a root-cause writeup. Push back on yourself if your first hypothesis feels too convenient.\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"fun_ending\": {\n    \"headline\": \"Claude confidently blamed a 'mini-model hallucination' for failing tests — until the user pushed back and the real culprit turned out to be an unpinned Codex CLI version\",\n    \"detail\": \"During a Codex E2E CI debugging session, Claude initially misdiagnosed a regression by blaming the AI model itself, but user skepticism led to discovering the actual cause: a version pin issue.\"\n  },\n  \"at_a_glance\": {\n    \"whats_work