h would have prevented the trailing-slash and SDK-drift issues you hit. And for debugging, expect to delegate full reproduce-from-telemetry → write failing test → bisect-to-green loops.\"\n  }\n}\n\nReport URL: file:///Users/soph/.claude/usage-data/report.html\nHTML file: /Users/soph/.claude/usage-data/report.html\nFacets directory: /Users/soph/.claude/usage-data/facets\n\nAt-a-glance summary (for your context only — the user has not seen any output yet):\n# Claude Code Insights\n\n575 sessions total · 33 analyzed · 392 messages · 641h · 63 commits\n2026-04-21 to 2026-04-27\n\n## At a Glance\n\n**What's working:** You run a tight review-driven loop: request a branch review, apply focused fixes, then ask for a re-review after polish. This pattern showed up across your auth token work, dispatch wizard migration, and checkpoint refactor, and it consistently produces clean commits with regression tests rather than monolithic drops. You also ground debugging in real telemetry — pulling Honeycomb and DB queries to verify diagnoses before shipping — which is why so many sessions land cleanly. See _Impressive Things You Did_.\n\n**What's hindering you:** On Claude's side: it tends to commit to a first theory too confidently (mini-model hallucination, error-path theory) and over-engineers initial implementations with unnecessary abstraction layers, plus mechanical slips like the wrong Go time format or schema drift slip through to review. On your side: you often accept that first diagnosis or implementation before asking for verification, and scope boundaries aren't always set up front — which is how /simplify wandered into a sibling repo and how trailing-slash routes and lost nolint comments made it to CI. See _Where Things Go Wrong_.\n\n**Quick wins to try:** Build a custom Skill for your PR review pattern that bakes in a pre-handoff checklist (lint round-trip, schema parity, CI format check, route shape) so mechanical errors get caught before you see them. Wire up MCP ser